WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.640
A practical CSF 2.0 operating routine starts with scope and context.

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:10.580
Define what is being assessed, why it matters, who owns it, what assumptions apply,

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.960
which suppliers or dependencies are involved, and what risk decisions

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:17.180
the organization needs to support.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:22.680
Then use Govern to clarify strategy, policy, roles, oversight, accountability,

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:24.820
and the connection to enterprise risk.

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:28.240
Use the six Functions as a shared risk language.

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:29.980
Govern sets direction.

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:36.040
Identify builds understanding of assets, data, suppliers, business context,

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:38.500
vulnerabilities, and risk.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:41.200
Protect selects safeguards to reduce risk.

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:45.100
Detect supports timely discovery and analysis.

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:48.280
Respond guides action during cybersecurity events.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:51.060
Recover supports restoration and resilience.

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:55.120
These Functions should be managed together, not as a one-time sequence.

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:58.140
Build Current and Target Profiles to guide improvement.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:01.560
The Current Profile should describe present outcomes with evidence.

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:06.820
The Target Profile should describe desired outcomes based on objectives, risk tolerance,

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:09.680
requirements, and threat context.

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:12.940
Comparing the two creates a prioritized improvement plan.

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:18.280
Tiers, Informative References, Implementation Examples, Quick Start Guides,

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:23.300
Community Profiles, and tools can help explain rigor, connect to other guidance,

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:25.040
and move from outcomes

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:26.700
to practical action.

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:32.440
The core message is simple: CSF 2.0 is a flexible risk management framework,

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:36.020
not a magic shield, certification, or universal checklist.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:40.320
It works best when it becomes a continuous operating routine for communication,

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:44.700
prioritization, ownership, evidence, and improvement.

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:49.160
Used honestly, it helps organizations understand cybersecurity risk,

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:52.020
make better decisions, and improve over time.
